    {"id": "S1", "url": "https://www.usgs.gov/special-topics/water-science-school/science/water-density", "checked_at": "2026-11-01",
     "title": "USGS Water Science School, Water Density: ice is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice cubes float; in most substances solids are more dense; ice molecules align in a regular lattice and are more spread out; the density of ice is about 90 percent that of water; about 10 percent of an ice cube (or iceberg) is above the water line; if ice were denser it would sink",
     "label": {"de": "USGS Water Science School", "en": "USGS Water Science School"}},
    {"id": "S2", "url": "https://courses.worldcampus.psu.edu/welcome/biol011/lesson02_06.html", "checked_at": "2026-11-01",
     "title": "Penn State World Campus, BIOL 011, Water: as water freezes, hydrogen bonds set the molecules a certain distance apart, farther than in liquid water, so ice is less dense and floats; usually density increases from liquid to solid; organisms can live under the ice at around 4 degrees; if ice were more dense, water would freeze from the bottom up",
     "label": {"de": "Penn State (Biologie)", "en": "Penn State (biology)"}}
  ],
  "de": {
    "title": "Warum schwimmt Eis oben? – Plümo taucht den Eiswürfel unter",
